        "code": "// Day 52 Part 3: Wasm Linear Memory Management & Zero-Copy Pipeline\nclass WasmLinearMemoryPipeline {\n  private memory: WebAssembly.Memory;\n\n  constructor(initialPages: number = 1) {\n    this.memory = new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: initialPages });\n  }\n\n  writeFloats(offset: number, data: number[]): void {\n    const view = new Float32Array(this.memory.buffer, offset, data.length);\n    view.set(data);\n  }\n\n  readFloats(offset: number, length: number): number[] {\n    const view = new Float32Array(this.memory.buffer, offset, length);\n    return Array.from(view);\n  }\n\n  getBufferByteLength(): number {\n    return this.memory.buffer.byteLength;\n  }\n}\n\nconst pipeline = new WasmLinearMemoryPipeline(1); // 1 page = 64KB = 65536 bytes\npipeline.writeFloats(0, [1.5, 2.75, 3.125, 4.0]);\nconst readBack = pipeline.readFloats(0, 4);\n\nconsole.log('Wasm Linear Memory Page Size (bytes):', pipeline.getBufferByteLength());\nconsole.log('Zero-Copy Float32 View Values:', readBack.join(', '));\nconsole.log('Wasm Linear Memory Management Verified: True');",
        "example": "Imagine a collaborative workshop shared by an English architect (JavaScript) and a Swiss precision toolmaker (WebAssembly). In the center of the room sits a heavy steel drafting workbench with numbered measurement grids (the Shared Linear Memory). Instead of the architect drawing a complex gear design on paper, mailing the paper through postal mail, and waiting for the toolmaker to redraw it, the architect simply lays the metal workpiece directly onto grid coordinate zero of the shared workbench. The toolmaker walks over, machines the gear with micrometer precision, and steps back. The finished gear is immediately sitting right there on the workbench, ready for the architect without a single second lost to transit.",
