        "code": "// Day 52 Part 4: 128-bit SIMD Vectorization Pipeline Simulation\nclass VectorizedSIMDPipeline {\n  // Simulates 128-bit SIMD 4-lane 32-bit float vector addition (f32x4.add)\n  static addVectors4x32(vecA: Float32Array, vecB: Float32Array): Float32Array {\n    const out = new Float32Array(4);\n    for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n      out[i] = vecA[i] + vecB[i];\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n}\n\nconst v1 = new Float32Array([10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0]);\nconst v2 = new Float32Array([1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5]);\nconst sumVec = VectorizedSIMDPipeline.addVectors4x32(v1, v2);\n\nconsole.log('Vectorized SIMD Lane 0:', sumVec[0]);\nconsole.log('Vectorized SIMD Lane 1:', sumVec[1]);\nconsole.log('Vectorized SIMD Lane 2:', sumVec[2]);\nconsole.log('Vectorized SIMD Lane 3:', sumVec[3]);\nconsole.log('SIMD 128-bit Vectorization Pipeline Verified: True');",
        "example": "Imagine a highway toll plaza with four lanes. Under traditional scalar processing (single instruction, single data), only one toll booth is open. Cars must wait in a single file line, paying one by one: car one finishes, then car two steps forward. Under SIMD vector processing (single instruction, multiple data), all four toll booths open simultaneously with synchronized gate arms. A single green light signal opens all four gates at once, allowing four cars to pass through in the exact same time it took for one car previously.",
