        "part": 4,
        "say": [
          "Welcome to Part 4 of Day 56. Beyond routing, singleton negotiation, and event buses, enterprise micro-frontends must guarantee visual integrity: CSS Style Isolation, Shadow DOM Encapsulation, and Design System Governance.",
          "Think of style isolation like acoustic soundproofing between adjoining apartments in a luxury high-rise condominium. If two neighbors share a porous, uninsulated drywall partition, music played in Apartment A reverberates into Apartment B, disrupting sleep and causing furious arguments. To ensure tranquility, builders install heavy acoustic insulation, floating concrete floors, and double-glazed soundproof glass. What happens inside Apartment A stays completely isolated within Apartment A, allowing both households to enjoy their living spaces without interference.",
          "In web browsers, the Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) engine is global by default. If Team Checkout writes a CSS selector like `button { background-color: #0066cc; padding: 12px; }`, that global rule cascades through the entire DOM tree, inadvertently turning every button in the Catalog micro-frontend, the Navigation header, and the Footer into blue 12px buttons. Even worse, if two teams define conflicting CSS class names\u2014such as `.container`, `.card`, or `.title`\u2014whichever CSS stylesheet was downloaded last by the network wins the cascade war, causing bizarre, unpredictable layout glitches that change from one page load to the next.",
          "To conquer global CSS pollution, enterprise micro-frontend architectures implement multi-layered style isolation strategies: CSS Modules with unique compile-time hash scoping, CSS-in-JS libraries (such as Emotion or Styled Components) with runtime class generation, Web Components with Shadow DOM encapsulation, or CSS Data-Attribute Scoping. In Shadow DOM architectures, a remote component is mounted inside a Shadow Root (`element.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })`). The browser's layout engine treats the shadow root as an impermeable barrier: CSS stylesheets declared inside the shadow tree cannot leak out, and global page styles cannot leak in.",
          "However, complete Shadow DOM encapsulation introduces trade-offs: global design system themes, typography fonts, and CSS variables must be deliberately pierced through custom CSS custom properties (variables) like `--brand-primary` or inherited font properties. Alternatively, enterprise systems utilize Container Scoping: during build time or runtime mounting, the host container wraps the remote markup in a unique attribute container (`data-mfe-container='checkoutWidget'`) and prefixes all component CSS selectors with this attribute boundary.",
          "Furthermore, design system governance teams provide pre-compiled, versioned Design System Tokens (JSON/CSS variables) defining standardized spacing, color palettes, elevation shadows, and typography scales. Because all remote micro-frontends consume the exact same underlying design tokens, the end-user perceives a unified, coherent enterprise brand experience even though the interface is rendered by five independent engineering teams deploying on different cadences.",
          "For organizations adopting utility-first frameworks like Tailwind CSS across micro-frontends, preventing utility collisions requires prefix configuration. In `tailwind.config.js`, teams declare isolated prefixes: `prefix: 'tw-checkout-'` for the checkout remote, and `prefix: 'tw-nav-'` for the navigation shell. In addition, resetting default browser styles with preflight stylesheets must be strictly scoped or disabled inside remotes; otherwise, a sub-app's reset stylesheet will overwrite root heading margins and list styling across the host application.",
          "Moreover, CSS custom properties (variables) provide the ideal bridge between style isolation and brand consistency. Because CSS variables automatically penetrate Shadow DOM and container boundaries through CSS inheritance, the host shell can define brand primitives like `--color-primary: #10b981` at the root `:root` level. Both host elements and nested remote micro-frontends access these CSS variables natively while keeping their local class selectors completely insulated from global pollution. Automated visual regression pipelines in CI/CD run Playwright snapshot diffs on every pull request, ensuring zero unintended style creep across federated viewports.",
          "In the implementation below, we build the `StyleIsolationContainer`. We encapsulate component CSS rules within an attribute-scoped container namespace, mount dynamic markup safely, and verify that styles remain strictly confined to the component's designated boundary."
        ],
