        "code": "class DependencyGraph {\n  private adjacency = new Map<string, string[]>();\n\n  addNode(name: string, deps: string[] = []): void {\n    this.adjacency.set(name, deps);\n  }\n\n  getExecutionOrder(): string[] {\n    const visited = new Set<string>();\n    const order: string[] = [];\n\n    const visit = (node: string) => {\n      if (visited.has(node)) return;\n      visited.add(node);\n      for (const dep of this.adjacency.get(node) || []) {\n        visit(dep);\n      }\n      order.push(node);\n    };\n\n    for (const node of this.adjacency.keys()) visit(node);\n    return order;\n  }\n}\n\nconst graph = new DependencyGraph();\ngraph.addNode(\"web-client\", [\"ui-kit\", \"api-client\"]);\ngraph.addNode(\"api-client\", [\"types\"]);\ngraph.addNode(\"ui-kit\", [\"types\"]);\ngraph.addNode(\"types\", []);\n\nconst buildOrder = graph.getExecutionOrder();\nconsole.log(`[Monorepo Graph] Topological Build Order: ${buildOrder.join(\" -> \")}`);",
        "example": "Organizing a master-planned commercial building where shared plumbing and power grids are maintained centrally while individual offices retain private entry.",
        "output": "[Monorepo Graph] Topological Build Order: types -> ui-kit -> api-client -> web-client",
        "part": 3,
        "say": [
          "Once an architectural topology is selected, the Principal Engineer must design the Codebase Topography and Monorepo Build Graph. In modern high-scale enterprises, managing dozens of isolated Git repositories (the multi-repo pattern) introduces devastating operational fragmentation: duplicated helper libraries, drifting TypeScript types, divergent dependency versions across teams, and agonizing multi-repository pull request coordination just to rename a single shared database column. To restore engineering harmony and code reuse, industry leaders like Google, Meta, Microsoft, and Uber organize their codebases into Unified Monorepos powered by tools like Nx, Turborepo, and pnpm workspaces.",
          "Think of a multi-repo ecosystem like a neighborhood of ten separate houses, where every homeowner maintains their own personal lawnmower, tool shed, electrical generator, and water filtration system. If the municipal water standard changes, each homeowner must individually purchase new filters, schedule independent inspections, and hope their neighbor did not buy an incompatible fitting. A Monorepo, in contrast, is like a modern master-planned skyscraper connected to a central utility grid. A single central maintenance team manages the shared plumbing, electrical, and HVAC infrastructure; when a utility pipe is upgraded, every resident immediately benefits from identical water pressure and water quality with zero divergent drift.",
          "However, an unconstrained monorepo quickly degenerates into a tangled, spaghetti-like morass if architectural boundaries are not strictly enforced. If a junior frontend engineer imports internal database entities directly into a React client component, or if a backend microservice directly references another service's private data access layer, module encapsulation collapses and cyclic dependencies paralyze the codebase. To prevent this entropy, the Principal Engineer establishes strict Project Boundaries and Module Dependency Rules enforced by automated linting (such as eslint-plugin-boundaries) and topological dependency analyzers.",
          "At the heart of modern monorepo build systems is the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) and Topological Sorting. When hundreds of applications and shared packages reside in one repository, running tests or building the entire codebase on every pull request is completely impossible\u2014build times would stretch into hours! The monorepo engine analyzes the file modification diff of a pull request, traverses the reverse dependency graph, and calculates the precise 'Affected' subset of packages that must be recompiled and tested.",
          "Furthermore, the build engine utilizes Topological Sorting to determine the optimal concurrent build execution order. Packages with zero upstream dependencies (such as shared core types or cryptographic utilities) are built first in parallel; dependent libraries (such as UI component design systems and API client SDKs) are scheduled next; and top-level web applications and backend server entrypoints are built last once all internal dependencies have compiled cleanly. Combined with Remote Build Caching, where identical build outputs are hashed and cached in cloud object storage, engineers achieve sub-minute build times across repositories containing millions of lines of code.",
          "Crucially, the monorepo architecture must enforce Strict API Contracts between internal packages through barrel exports and explicit package entry points defined in `package.json` exports maps. Internal implementation details of a shared package must remain private; external consumers may only bind against public interfaces and exported types. This encapsulation ensures that internal refactoring within shared utility libraries can proceed safely without triggering unexpected cascading breakage across hundreds of downstream consumer applications. When properly maintained, a monorepo functions as a harmonious, unified engine powering enterprise-scale velocity.",
          "In the TypeScript implementation below, we build a clean DependencyGraph engine. We model interconnected monorepo packages, detect topological relationships, and compute the exact linear build execution order required to compile dependent artifacts without cyclic collision.",
          "Study the output carefully: notice how the foundational types package is scheduled first, followed by downstream intermediate libraries (ui-kit and api-client), and finally the top-level web-client application. This topological discipline is what enables massive enterprise engineering organizations to scale seamlessly without build collisions."
        ],
        "title": "Monorepo Architecture & Build Topology: Nx, Turborepo, and Shared Code Boundary Enforcement"
